//? Title: Eytzinger Search: Actual Tree Placements and Probe Paths
//? Subtitle: Complete-tree rank, exact-match search and the limits of layout-based cost claims
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find an integer in a static sorted collection while changing how its search tree
is stored. Eytzinger order places a complete binary search tree breadth first in
one array. We begin with seven values and inspect a real three-probe lookup,
then retain the original ten-value example and its five queries. Native traces
record actual construction assignments and actual search transitions. Complete
and incomplete trees, duplicate keys, a hypothetical block model and explicit
capture limits distinguish element inspections from comparisons, cache misses
and elapsed time. No local search loop reconstructs an explanatory path.
)

\toc

> A seven-value tree and an actual lookup

For [1,3,5,7,9,11,13], the native layout is [7,3,11,1,5,9,13].
Using zero-based array positions, the root is zero and its children are
2i+1 and 2i+2 when those positions exist. Searching for five reads seven,
three, then five: positions zero, one, four. Equality is checked before
deciding which child to take. The returned index four belongs to the
layout, whereas the value's original sorted rank is two.

The program renders positions from actual assignment records and highlights
only the recorded search probes. Tree edges show the implicit array topology;
they do not replay the search. Gray nodes were not read by this query.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/eytzinger_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
#include <set>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto built=dm::algorithms::eytzinger_layout_trace({1,3,5,7,9,11,13});const auto walk=dm::algorithms::eytzinger_search_trace(built.layout,5);
  std::print("| probe | layout index | read value | decision | next index |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  std::set<std::size_t> visited;for(std::size_t j=0;j<walk.probes.size();++j){const auto& p=walk.probes[j];visited.insert(p.index);std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",j+1,p.index,p.value,p.direction==0?"match":p.direction<0?"left":"right",p.next_index);}
  std::print("\nTiny lookup: found {}; layout index {}; probes {}; captured records {}.\n",walk.result.found,walk.result.index,walk.result.probes,walk.captured_entries);
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(58,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.8;box.height=1.1;box.font_size=22;
  for(const auto& p:built.placements){const auto depth=std::bit_width(p.layout_index+1)-1;box.fill=visited.contains(p.layout_index)?dm::graphics::RGB{191,219,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{226,232,240};if(static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(p.layout_index)==walk.result.index)box.fill={187,247,208};g.node(std::to_string(p.layout_index),{2.*p.sorted_index,-2.3*depth},std::format("value {}\nindex {}",p.value,p.layout_index),box);}
  for(std::size_t i=1;i<built.layout.size();++i){dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.color=visited.contains(i)&&visited.contains((i-1)/2)?dm::graphics::RGB{37,99,235}:dm::graphics::RGB{148,163,184};stroke.width=visited.contains(i)?4:2;g.edge(std::to_string((i-1)/2),std::to_string(i),i%2?"left":"right",stroke);}
  g.text({6,2.2},"Actual lookup of five: 7 → 3 → 5",27).text({6,-6.7},"Layout index 4 differs from sorted rank 2",23).save_svg("build/eytzinger_tiny.svg");
  if(!walk.result.found||walk.result.index!=4||walk.result.probes!=3)throw std::runtime_error("tiny lookup failed");
}
