> What breadth-first packing actually promises

The first h complete levels occupy the contiguous prefix of 2^h−1 values.
A node's two existing children are adjacent. These are layout facts; a search
follows one child at a time and need not remain within one physical cache line.
Actual cache behavior also depends on element width, base alignment, line size,
working-set size, repeated queries, eviction, speculation and prefetching.

To visualize only the packing fact, the next program uses fifteen values and
hypothetical aligned blocks of four integers. It collects the first two actual
probes over all successful queries. Eytzinger uses positions 0,1,2, all in model
block zero; sorted binary search uses 3,7,11, in three model blocks. These sets
combine different queries. They are neither a single lookup's cache misses nor
a cache simulation, and the four-integer block is an illustrative assumption.
For example, all fifteen four-byte integers may fit in one aligned 64-byte line.

Khuong and Morin study RAM-resident lower-bound queries and tuned implementations,
including conditional moves and prefetching. Their results depend on sizes and
machines. Our equality-first exact-match kernel implements neither optimization,
and no reported speedup is transferred from that study. Building the layout costs
O(N); retain and reuse it across queries when evaluating that setup tradeoff.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/eytzinger_packing.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <bit>
#include <set>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<int> sorted(15);std::iota(sorted.begin(),sorted.end(),1);const auto l=dm::algorithms::eytzinger_layout(sorted);std::set<std::size_t> ei,bi,eb,bb;
  for(int key:sorted){const auto e=dm::algorithms::eytzinger_search_trace(l,key);const auto b=dm::algorithms::binary_search_trace(sorted,key);for(std::size_t j=0;j<std::min<std::size_t>(2,e.probes.size());++j){ei.insert(e.probes[j].index);eb.insert(e.probes[j].index/4);}for(std::size_t j=0;j<std::min<std::size_t>(2,b.probes.size());++j){bi.insert(b.probes[j].index);bb.insert(b.probes[j].index/4);}}
  std::print("Model: {} values; four integers per hypothetical aligned block; actual int width {} bytes.\n",sorted.size(),sizeof(int));std::print("Eytzinger first-two-probe positions:");for(auto i:ei)std::print(" {}",i);std::print("; model blocks {}.\n",eb.size());std::print("Sorted binary first-two-probe positions:");for(auto i:bi)std::print(" {}",i);std::print("; model blocks {}.\n",bb.size());
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(57,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.05;box.height=1.1;box.font_size=20;box.padding=6;const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colours{{219,234,254},{220,252,231},{254,243,199},{243,232,255}};
  for(int row=0;row<2;++row){const auto& values=row?sorted:l;const auto& selected=row?bi:ei;g.text({9.1,1.7-4.*row},row?"Sorted array: first-two-probe set spans three model blocks":"BFS layout: first-two-probe set lies in model block zero",25);for(std::size_t i=0;i<values.size();++i){box.fill=colours[i/4];box.border=selected.contains(i)?dm::graphics::RGB{220,38,38}:dm::graphics::RGB{148,163,184};g.node(std::format("{}:{}",row,i),{1.3*i,-4.*row},std::format("i={}\n{}",i,values[i]),box);}}
  g.text({9.1,3.6},"Hypothetical aligned blocks of four integers",27).text({9.1,-6.3},"Colored blocks and selected read sets are a model; no cache misses measured",23).save_svg("build/eytzinger_packing.svg");if(eb.size()!=1||bb.size()!=3)throw std::runtime_error("packing model failed");
}
